**Read:** Revisit the completed source, treatment, distribution, wastewater and industrial section studies. Use [Assessment of Treatment Plant Performance and Water Quality Data — von Sperling, Verbyla and Oliveira](../sources/Treatment-Plant-Performance-and-Water-Quality-Data.lingua#r_5PZG8VN422GHD657FBDR5KRWGS) for the evidence argument and the full manuals relevant to the chosen system.

An integrated study begins with a question about water and the people or processes receiving its service. The boundary can include abstraction, catchment effects, treatment, storage, delivery, collection, recovery and receiving environments. Every additional boundary needs a reason; the purpose is to explain the service, not to draw every industry in the world.

**Work:** Follow one source-to-use-to-return system over a declared period. Account for water, at least two meaningful constituents, energy, residuals, continuing labour and external supplies. Reconcile discrepancies before optimizing. Compare a technically plausible alternative and explain effects on upstream and downstream work.

The following three studies examine drinking-water delivery, wastewater treatment and industrial reuse. Work each at the depth needed to explain its central equations and evidence, then pursue at least one through a full independent advanced investigation. Their continuations meet at uncertainty and model comparison. The same Records can support other selections without requiring a special project structure.

**Return:** [Water, sanitation and industrial utilities](../Water-sanitation-and-industrial-utilities.lingua#r_7B9GRCJFHVBEEQEXKWCR2QMMPN).
